>
  <div class="bg-white min-h-screen">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">

      <p class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mb-4" style="color:var(--color-amber);">
        <a href="/thoughts" class="hover:opacity-70" style="color:var(--color-amber);">Thoughts</a>
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-[28px] font-semibold tracking-tight mb-3" style="color:#1a1a1a;">A Skull from Georgia</h1>
      <p class="text-[17px] leading-[1.6] mb-2" style="color:#666;">On the one category defined by what it is not, and the relic bolted to the front of it</p>
      <p class="text-[12px] mb-12" style="color:#999;">August 2026</p>

      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The line reads, in full: <span class="italic">White or Caucasian (Not Hispanic or Latino)</span>. It sits in a dropdown, fourth or fifth down depending on the vendor, between two other lines that have been revised more recently and more carefully. Roughly three in five Americans select it. Almost none of them read it, because there is nothing in it that looks like news.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Read it anyway, slowly, as a piece of writing. It has three components and they come from three different centuries and three incompatible systems of thought. <span class="italic">Not Hispanic or Latino</span> is a subtraction, and what it subtracts is a category that did not exist before 1977. <span class="italic">White</span> is a color word, doing duty as a proper noun, describing a population whose actual skin tones run most of the available range. <span class="italic">Caucasian</span> is a mountain range, and behind the mountain range is a specific dead woman's skull, and behind the skull is an aesthetic judgment made by a German anatomist in 1795 about which of the skulls in his cabinet he found the most beautiful.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the whole label. A negation, a color and a compliment paid to a corpse. It is the only entry on the form assembled that way, it is the largest one, and it is the only one left substantially alone through fifty years of fastidious revision of everything around it. Those three facts are the same fact, and the fact is not about race. It is about who has to be listened to.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/region-30n-60w/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1024"
          height="1024"
          alt="Hard-edged abstract painting of overlapping circles in red, orange, blue, yellow and black crossed by fine ruled lines on a cream ground"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto"
          style="background:#f5f5f5;"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-3 text-[11px] leading-[1.6]" style="color:#999;">
          30N 60W, western North Atlantic, rendered from that day's atmospheric data.
          From <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/region-30n-60w/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70 break-all" style="color:var(--color-amber);">art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/region-30n-60w</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5" style="color:var(--color-amber);">The Prettiest One in the Cabinet</h2>
      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Johann Friedrich Blumenbach taught medicine at Göttingen and collected skulls, some sixty of them, acquired through the ordinary channels of eighteenth-century natural history, which is to say through soldiers, physicians, travellers and the Russian court. In the third edition of his treatise on the natural varieties of mankind, published in 1795, he settled on five varieties, and for the one that included Europeans he coined a word that had not previously existed in this sense. He called them Caucasian.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          He explained why, and the explanation is the part that has not survived into the dropdown. He named the variety after the Caucasus because a skull in his possession, from a Georgian woman, was in his judgment the most beautiful specimen he had, and because he believed, on the strength of that beauty, that the region was likely to have been the original seat of the species from which the others had since departed. That is the argument. It is not an argument from measurement, or from lineage, or from anything a modern person would recognise as evidence. It is a judgment of taste, elevated to a claim about origins, and then attached as a name to several hundred million people.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Stephen Jay Gould made this point in <span class="italic">The Geometer of Race</span>, along with a second one usually dropped when the first is quoted. Blumenbach was, for his moment, a decent and generous man. He insisted the varieties shaded continuously into one another with no sharp boundary anywhere, he wrote against the notion that Africans were intellectually inferior and collected their published works to prove it, and he would have been appalled by most of what the next century did with his vocabulary. His sin was not malice. It was the smaller and more durable one of arranging a continuum around a favourite, so that everything else became a degree of departure from it. Once you have a centre you have a periphery, and the periphery is where the nineteenth century went to work.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no version of this history in which Caucasian is a technical term. It was never a measurement. The craniometry that came later, and that did claim to measure, is precisely the body of work that has been discarded in its entirety, including by the people who were once its keenest practitioners. Strip out the parts that were disproved and the parts that were abandoned and here is the residue, the whole of it: a word meaning resembling a skull an anatomist thought was pretty, named for a mountain range where none of the people it describes have ever lived. Three in five Americans certify themselves against it before lunch.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5" style="color:var(--color-amber);">The Words That Were Removed</h2>
      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The first hypocrisy needs no theory at all. It is a comparison of two lists. American officialdom has spent fifty years removing exactly this kind of language, category by category, and has generally been right to. The Census retired the word Negro from its forms after the 2010 count, on the reasonable ground that it belonged to a vocabulary the described population had stopped using about itself two generations earlier. In 2016 a statute struck Oriental and Negro from the body of federal law outright, replacing them with terms people actually use. The category once labelled Asian or Pacific Islander was split in two, because the Pacific Islander population objected that being averaged into a much larger group made them statistically invisible. American Indian acquired Alaska Native. Hispanic acquired or Latino, and then in a great many private systems acquired or Latinx as well. Every one of these changes came from the same argument, made by the people described: the word is an artifact of somebody else's taxonomy, we did not choose it, and we would like a different one.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Now hold that record of scrupulousness next to the fact that the single most conspicuously nineteenth-century word in the entire apparatus, the one that is literally the founding coinage of racial typology, is still sitting in production on tens of thousands of application forms in the year 2026, glued to the largest box with the word <span class="italic">or</span>.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          It is not there because anyone defends it. Nobody defends it. No geneticist uses it, no anthropologist has used it approvingly in fifty years, and no ordinary person says it about themselves without irony. Nobody at a party announces that they are Caucasian. The word survives in exactly two registers: the police blotter, where it describes a suspect at a distance, and the job application, where it asks you to describe yourself the way the blotter would. In the first a stranger is guessing at you from across the street. In the second you have been handed the stranger's vocabulary and asked to do the guessing on his behalf, unpaid, while hoping to be hired.
        </p>
        <p>
          The federal instrument, to be fair to it, does not say Caucasian and never has. The 1977 standard said White and the 2024 revision still says White. The word is an in-house embellishment, added by employers and vendors somewhere in the drafting, and it has propagated by copying. That is worth sitting with. Nobody wrote it. Nobody adopted it. Nobody has ever approved it in a meeting. It got into your employer's system the way a virus gets into a school, and it stays for one reason: the only force that has ever removed a word from these forms is a complaint from the people the word is about, and this box does not complain.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which tells you what the fifty years of careful revision were actually made of. Not a growing scruple about naming people badly. A queue. Every correction was earned, in each case by somebody organising, drafting, testifying and waiting, and every correction stopped precisely at the edge of the constituency that fought for it. Where nobody was pushing, the eighteenth century simply stayed on the page, in bold, in production, load-tested by nothing, untouched. If you want to know whether an institution has a principle or a complaints department, find the place where a principle would apply and no one is complaining, and look at what is sitting there.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/arctic-70n-20e/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1024"
