          class="underline hover:opacity-70 break-all" style="color:var(--color-amber);">art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-08-12-060044/arctic-70n-20e</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5" style="color:var(--color-amber);">Defined by Subtraction</h2>
      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The front half of the phrase is stranger than the back half, and much less remarked on. Every other line on the form is constituted positively. Black or African American names an origin. Asian names an origin. American Indian or Alaska Native names an origin, and in the more careful instruments names a tribal enrollment behind it. Non-Hispanic White names an origin and then removes part of it. Alone among the categories, it is defined in part by what its members are not, and the thing they are not is a classification that was manufactured, by legislation in 1976 and by administrative directive the following year, out of populations that had not previously regarded themselves as a common kind.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          So the arithmetic runs like this. In 1977 the government invents Hispanic. Having invented it, it must then subtract it from the others, because the new category was defined as an ethnicity that cuts across the race question rather than sitting inside it. The subtraction lands hardest on White, since that is where most of the overlap is. And the residue of that subtraction, a remainder produced by an operation performed on a category that did not exist a decade earlier, is the box that three in five Americans now tick, and the reference group in essentially every published statistic about the country.
        </p>
        <p>
          A category that is a remainder is not, on that account, empty. But it is worth being honest about what it is doing. When a regression reports an outcome for non-Hispanic Whites, that number is not a measurement of a people. It is a measurement of whoever is left after two prior definitional decisions have been applied, both of which were made by committee, one of which was made in living memory, and either of which could be revised on a Tuesday. And being the remainder has a specific consequence that being a category does not. The remainder is the unmarked case. It is what the other numbers are differences from, which means it never has to be argued for, never gets audited, and never appears in the sentence at all. It is the zero on the ruler, and nobody inspects the zero.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5" style="color:var(--color-amber);">What the Box Is Wired To</h2>
      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Fine, says a reasonable person: the word is silly, the boundary is arbitrary, and none of it matters, because the answer goes into a filing cabinet and no one acts on it. Then here is the direct answer. Categories built on exactly this footing, self-reported, uncriterioned, drawn by committee, have been wired into decisions with bodies on the other end, and were still wired in within the last five years.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The clearest case is kidney function. For roughly two decades the standard equations used to estimate glomerular filtration rate from a blood test, the number that determines whether you are referred to a nephrologist, whether you qualify for a transplant waiting list and when your name goes on it, contained a coefficient that adjusted the result upward for any patient recorded as Black. Not for a measured biological variable. For the census box. The effect of a higher estimated filtration rate is that the patient looks healthier than they are, and the referral comes later. A national task force convened by the two principal kidney societies concluded in 2021 that the coefficient should go, and it went, and in 2023 the organ allocation system began adjusting the accrued waiting times of Black candidates who had been held back by the old equation. A category with no criterion, entered on a form, multiplied by a number, subtracted from someone's place in a queue for an organ, for twenty years, and then corrected in arrears.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          It is not an isolated instance. Pulmonary function testing carried race-specific reference equations on the same logic, so that a given measured lung capacity was scored against a different expected normal depending on the box, with consequences for disability determinations and workplace exposure claims, until the American Thoracic Society moved to race-neutral equations in 2023. In 2005 the Food and Drug Administration approved a heart failure drug with a label restricted to self-identified black patients, the first race-labelled drug in the country, on trial evidence that did not include a comparison arm of anyone else. In each case the input was a self-reported administrative category descended from the same 1977 directive, and in each case the professional bodies that had built on it eventually concluded, in public, that the variable was standing in for something else: for exposure, for income, for insurance, for the accumulated effects of being treated as a member of the category. Which is the point. It was never measuring a kind of body. It was measuring a history of treatment, badly, while being read as biology.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notice where the residual sits in all of that. In every one of those equations the adjustment was applied to somebody else, which means the box under discussion here was the reference value: the normal the correction was defined against. A word coined for a skull an anatomist admired became the centre of a continuum, and two centuries later, in a clinic, the numbers were still being written as departures from that centre. Blumenbach at least said plainly why he had arranged the cabinet that way.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5" style="color:var(--color-amber);">Twice, Within a Year</h2>
      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The Supreme Court settled whether the boundary is drawn or found, twice, in consecutive terms, contradicting itself so plainly that the second opinion has to spend several pages explaining why the first one does not count.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          In 1922, in <span class="italic">Ozawa</span>, a Japanese applicant for naturalisation argued that he was white, on grounds including that his skin was in fact paler than that of many Europeans. The Court held that white meant Caucasian, that Caucasian was a matter for scientific authority, and that a Japanese man was not one. The reasoning is explicitly typological: the statute is read through Blumenbach's vocabulary, and the vocabulary decides.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Three months later, in <span class="italic">Thind</span>, a Punjabi applicant made the obvious move. The anthropology of the day classified high-caste North Indians as Aryan and therefore Caucasian. He was, on the Court's own stated test, exactly what it had said was required. The Court held that he was not white. It held that the word in the statute must be read in accordance with the understanding of the common man, that scientific classification was not controlling after all, and that whatever the anthropologists said, ordinary people did not regard this applicant as one of them.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Two rulers, again, giving different lengths, and this time in the same courtroom within a single year. Notice what the second opinion had to do. It did not distinguish the first case or refine it. It threw away the test the same nine men had announced three months earlier, because the test had produced the wrong man, and replaced it with the understanding of the common man, which is to say with whatever the answer needed to be. The category has no criterion. It has a job, which is sorting admissions, and it will wear whichever criterion gets the sorting done, dropping the science the instant the science lets the wrong person in. Blumenbach's word was never the reason for anything. It was the coat of paint, and in 1923 it came off in a single term, in public, in the United States Reports.
        </p>
        <p>
          And the boundary kept moving after the courts stopped writing about it. The 1977 directive defined White as origins in the original peoples of Europe, North Africa or the Middle East, which meant that for forty-seven years an Egyptian, a Lebanese and a Persian were officially White in the United States, whatever anyone in the building thought when they walked in. In March 2024 the standard was revised, a Middle Eastern or North African category was created, and several million people ceased to be White by memorandum, having done nothing whatever. That is a bird promoted to full species while asleep, with the difference, as ever, that these ones can read the notice.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5" style="color:var(--color-amber);">The Load-Bearing Or</h2>
      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Which raises the question the label invites and has never had to answer: are the two terms mutually exclusive, coextensive, or neither. The word joining them makes a promise, that they pick out the same set and the second is a courtesy to readers who prefer it. The promise fails in both directions at once.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          There are exactly two ways to pry a disjunction apart: find something on the left that is not on the right, and something on the right that is not on the left. The two cases just described are those two things. Thind was Caucasian and not white. Ozawa was paler than most Europeans and not Caucasian. Both lost, and that is the finding. Whichever half of the phrase you satisfy, the other half remains available to refuse you. It is not a disjunction. It is a conjunction wearing an <span class="italic">or</span>, and the second term exists to be produced when the first one has gone the wrong way.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The front of the label misbehaves in the opposite manner, and more quietly, because it excludes people who are inside it. Race and ethnicity have been asked as two separate questions since 1977, deliberately orthogonal, so that a person may be Hispanic and of any race. Millions of Americans accordingly answer White to the race question and Hispanic to the ethnicity question, both truthfully, both by the instrument's own rules. The compound label then removes them. <span class="italic">Non-Hispanic White</span> does not name the White population. It names the White population minus some of the White people, subtracted on an axis the form has just finished insisting is a different axis. A set that deletes its own members using a criterion it has declared independent is not a category in any sense a logician would recognise. It is a bookkeeping convenience that has been read aloud so often it now sounds like a description of persons.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Now the sharper half: can you deny yourself one of these and be wrong. The apparatus answers no, loudly, on every page. You are the authority on what you are and the institution is only writing it down.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The instructions answer yes. The EEO-1 reporting guidance states that self-identification is the preferred method, and then addresses what happens when an employee declines to provide it, which is that the employer must file the report regardless and may rely on employment records or on observer identification. Observer identification. If you decline, someone in the building looks at you and types the answer in. Blumenbach at least had the specimen in front of him and wrote down his reasoning; the observer has a glance across an open-plan floor, a filing deadline, and no obligation to tell you what he decided. Refusal is not a null. It is a blank the institution is instructed to fill by eye.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Which means the answer to the question is worse than either yes or no. You cannot be wrong, because there is no fact of the matter to be wrong about, and you can be overruled, because there is a filing deadline. The category is simultaneously unfalsifiable and involuntary. Deny it and you are corrected by a stranger's glance; assert it and no one checks, because there is nothing to check against. Both of those are the behaviour of a category that is about how you are seen, dressed in the grammar of a category about what you are.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          And you can be wrong in both directions at once, which is the part people who have lived near the boundary already know and everyone else finds hard to credit. The person with one grandparent from somewhere is told by one audience that they are claiming something that is not theirs, and by another that they are disowning something that is, on identical facts, in the same week, each correction delivered with the flat confidence of someone pointing out a typo. Both cannot be right. Both can easily be wrong. The only way to make sense of a claim that can fail in opposite directions simultaneously is to notice that it was never a claim about the person. It was a claim about the audience, and the audience changes rooms.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keep hold of where this is being asked. Not a census form arriving by post from a statistical agency with no view about you, not an intake sheet at a clinic that owes you a duty of care. A job application. The party posing the question is the party deciding whether you eat, and it is posing it in the same session, in the same interface, ten fields after your salary expectation. It presents a menu you did not write, containing a disjunction that has never once been resolved in favour of the applicant, and a subtraction performed on an axis it has just told you is unrelated. It asks you to certify yourself against that string. It tells you the answer is voluntary and that no one will see it, and its own reporting instructions provide that if you decline, somebody there may look at you and write one in. There is no version of that transaction in which the applicant is the authority on anything. The only party in the room with a settled view of what you are is the one holding the job.
        </p>
      </div>

      <RelatedEssay essays={[{
        href: '/thoughts/decline-to-self-identify',
        title: 'Decline to Self-Identify',
        blurb: 'On the questionnaire that invents the person it counts, and the one box on it that cannot',
      }]} />

      <h2 class="text-[9px] tracking-[0.25em] uppercase mt-12 mb-5" style="color:var(--color-amber);">Resolution Follows Politics</h2>
      <div class="text-[15px] leading-[1.85]" style="color:#444;" data-ripple-text>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The second hypocrisy is about how much detail the instrument will collect, and where. A good modern form asks Asian applicants to specify, and offers Chinese, Filipino, Asian Indian, Vietnamese, Korean, Japanese and a write-in. It separates Native Hawaiian from Samoan from Chamorro. It asks American Indian respondents to name the enrolled or principal tribe. This is right, and it is right for a reason that was argued for and won: aggregation destroys information, and the destroyed information was in each case load-bearing for someone's understanding of their own position.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Then the same form takes about fifty-eight percent of the country, a set running from Sicilians to Swedes to Bosnians to Irish Catholics to Ashkenazi Jews to eleventh-generation Yankees, with wildly different arrival dates, wildly different histories under the very statutes at issue, and in several cases a living memory of not having counted as White at all, and puts them on one line next to a mountain range in the Caucasus.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Notice the shape of the rule. The instrument's resolution is highest exactly where the political stakes are highest and lowest exactly where they are lowest. That is not a fact about the population. Nothing about Poles and Portuguese makes them more alike than Koreans and Cambodians. It is a fact about which groups have organised to demand a line of their own, which is a perfectly legitimate way for a democracy to allocate attention, and which should simply be said out loud rather than presented as a taxonomy. A classification whose granularity is set by lobbying is a record of lobbying.
        </p>
        <p>
          In fairness, the 2024 revision addresses this on paper. It requires detailed collection for every category, including White, with examples such as German, Irish, English, Italian and Polish, and it applies the same requirement to the new Middle Eastern or North African line. Whether it survives contact with the vendors is the open question, and the honest early answer is that the applicant tracking system in front of you this afternoon almost certainly still shows one line, and that line very possibly still says Caucasian.
        </p>
      </div>

